% ============================================================================
% kingdoms — each biological kingdom of life mapped to the example organisms
% this table carries from the source's "Examples:" lines (adj-facts-stdlib,
% BIOLOGY). NOT every example the source lists: see the under-coverage note
% below (`rusts`, and the sixth kingdom Archaea).
% ============================================================================
% One of the very first ideas a biology learner meets: every living thing on
% Earth is sorted into a small number of top-level KINGDOMS, and each kingdom
% has familiar example organisms that live in it. "Animals are in Animalia;
% flowering plants are in Plantae; mushrooms are Fungi; amoebas are Protista;
% cyanobacteria are Bacteria." Before you can reason about an organism at all,
% it helps to know WHICH kingdom it belongs to. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? kingdom_example(fungi, $Example)      % mushrooms ; yeast ; molds
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `kingdom_example(kingdom, example)` carrying the table's citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns EVERY
% example WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% word that is not one of these kingdoms (it never invents an example). Each
% kingdom is deliberately ONE-TO-MANY: the source names several example
% organisms per kingdom, so `kingdom_example(animalia, $Example)` binds
% `$Example` to EACH of them in turn, just like `flame_color.adj`'s many-to-one
% `red` (strontium ; lithium) already established the reverse shape in this
% same stdlib.
%
% Each kingdom below and every example THIS TABLE CARRIES for it, as a truth
% table (the source lists one more example, `rusts`, and a whole sixth kingdom,
% neither of which this table carries — see below):
%
%     kingdom     example                a beginner's cue
%     ---------   --------------------   ----------------------------------
%     animalia    humans                 the animal kingdom
%     animalia    birds
%     animalia    crustaceans
%     animalia    sponges
%     plantae     flowers                the plant kingdom
%     plantae     grasses
%     plantae     conifers
%     plantae     ferns
%     plantae     mosses
%     fungi       mushrooms              the fungus kingdom
%     fungi       yeast
%     fungi       molds
%     protista    amoebas                single-celled eukaryotes
%     protista    diatoms
%     protista    dinoflagellates
%     protista    ciliates
%     protista    slime_molds
%     protista    single_celled_algae
%     bacteria    cyanobacteria          the prokaryote kingdom
%     bacteria    gram_positive_bacteria
%     bacteria    gram_negative_bacteria
%     bacteria    actinobacteria
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind an example and recall its kingdom:
%
%     ? kingdom_example($Kingdom, mushrooms)   % fungi
%
% A word that is NOT one of these kingdoms — `virus`, `mineral`, `rock` — is
% deliberately NOT a row, so a kingdom recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing an example. (Viruses are a classic example: they are not placed in
% any of these kingdoms, so the table honestly abstains on `virus`.) That
% honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner
% on: it answers exactly the kingdom→example pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `example` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase snake_case atom, chosen
% to be an organism the source EXPLICITLY lists for that kingdom — never one the
% source does not support. (Where the source's own phrase is two or more words
% — "slime molds", "single-celled algae", "Gram-positive
% ... bacteria" — the atom joins them with underscores.)
%
% NOT every value is a contiguous substring of its span, and the ellipsis
% above is where that breaks. "Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria"
% states two examples by DISTRIBUTING one head noun; neither
% `gram_positive_bacteria` nor the phrase "Gram-positive bacteria" occurs
% contiguously on the page. Those two rows rest on reading the conjunction
% as distributive — a reading, not a quotation. The removed
% `multicellular_algae` row failed a weaker test than that: its phrase is
% absent under ANY reading, occurring zero times on the page.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (every kingdom→example pair below was read out of a single
% fetched Science Notes "Kingdoms of Life in Biology" page). This paragraph
% used to end "and any pair whose example could not be verified there would
% have been dropped" — which this file disproves: one such pair shipped for a
% full cycle and is removed below. The guarantee was never enforced by
% anything; the per-row spans and their pins are the enforcement.
%
% Each pair now carries the verbatim "Examples:" span that
% states it AS ITS OWN `source` (RS-5e per-row provenance, #14986), rather than
% being listed here where no answer could reach it:
%
%   animalia  "Examples: Humans, birds, crustaceans, sponges"
%   plantae   "Examples: Flowers, grasses, conifers, ferns, mosses"
%   fungi     "Examples: Mushrooms, yeast, molds, rusts"
%   protista  "Examples: Amoebas, diatoms, dinoflagellates, ciliates, slime
%              molds, single-celled algae"
%   bacteria  "Examples: Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria,
%              cyanobacteria, actinobacteria"  (the "and"-joined pair names two
%              distinct, literal examples in the same list -- not an "or"-joined
%              alternative categorization -- so each is its own row)
%
% ONE ROW WAS REMOVED: `plantae -> multicellular_algae`. Re-measured 2026-09-13
% UTC with a raw fetch, the page's plantae line reads "Examples: Flowers,
% grasses, conifers, ferns, mosses" -- it does NOT contain "multicellular
% algae", and that phrase occurs ZERO times anywhere on the page — in the
% rendered text AND in the raw HTML, which no choice of extractor can move
% ("algae" occurs twice, both Protista, where the page actively places algae).
% A row the cited page does not state is exactly what this library exists not
% to ship, so it is gone and the recall abstains rather than asserting it.
%
% WHICH OF THREE CAUSES IS NOT DETERMINED HERE, and the note below is why it
% matters. The previous header recorded the plantae span WITH "multicellular
% algae" and said the lines were "WebFetch-verified (re-fetched the live page
% and confirmed every 'Examples:' line word-for-word AGAINST THE HEADER'S
% ORIGINAL TRANSCRIPTION)" [emphasis added] — quoted in full, because that
% trailing qualifier is load-bearing and an earlier draft dropped it. It says
% the old
% check compared the page against THIS FILE'S OWN prior transcription, which
% admits a third cause beside the obvious two: the page changed after the
% check; the summarizing fetcher misreported the line; or the line was never
% independently read, and the check confirmed the header against itself. The
% evidence to hand cannot separate them, so none is asserted. What IS
% settled: a raw fetch today does not contain the phrase, and a summarizing
% fetcher is not an instrument for a verbatim span.
%
% The fungi line also reads "Mushrooms, yeast, molds, rusts" today; the three
% shipped fungi rows are each stated by it, and `rusts` is simply a fourth
% example this table does not carry. The page also now describes a SIXTH
% kingdom, Archaea ("Examples: Methanogens, extreme halophiles, thermophiles,
% ammonia-oxidizing archaea"), which this table does not cover either. Both are
% under-coverage, not defects, and neither is expanded here.
%
% CENSUS, 2026-09-13 UTC, over the one page, rendered text (inline tags removed
% with no separator -- the page writes "<strong>Examples</strong>:", so a naive
% every-tag-becomes-a-space extractor yields "Examples : " and scores ZERO on
% its own positive control, which is how the first run of this harness failed):
% TWO counts, because they answer different questions and only one of them
% justifies deleting a row:
%
%   21 of the 23 rows shipped BEFORE this change are a CONTIGUOUS SUBSTRING
%      of their kingdom's line (hyphens and underscores normalised to spaces
%      on both sides). Of the 22 shipped after it: still 21.
%   22 of those 23 have every TOKEN of the atom present in that line.
%      Of the 22 shipped after it: 22.
%
% The two rows that differ are `plantae -> multicellular_algae` and
% `bacteria -> gram_positive_bacteria`. They are NOT the same case, and
% saying only "22 of 23" hid that:
%
%   gram_positive_bacteria — every token is on the line; the page writes
%     "Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria", distributing one head
%     noun across two examples. KEPT, on that distributive reading, which
%     the note above now states rather than implies.
%   multicellular_algae    — zero tokens-in-phrase, zero substring, and the
%     phrase occurs zero times anywhere on the page under any reading.
%     REMOVED.
%
% Hyphen normalisation cleared TWO of the first run's four apparent misses
% ("single-celled algae", "gram_negative_bacteria"), not three; the third,
% `gram_positive_bacteria`, is not a hyphen artifact and is the case above.
% Controls: a fabricated atom fails for every kingdom; a known-good atom passes.
%
% All spans are from the same page,
% https://sciencenotes.org/kingdoms-of-life-in-biology/ — a secondary science
% teaching resource, so `trust consensus` (not a primary .gov/.edu source).
% No row restates `locator` or `trust`: one page, one tier, and a row inherits
% every field it does not write.
%
% EXTENDED from 5 to 23 rows in an earlier cycle, now 22: the SAME already-cited
% page's own "Examples:" lines name the additional organisms — zero new source
% page needed. This was the fifth extend-pattern win in this loop's run (after
% animal-classes.adj, idiom-meaning.adj, plant-tropisms.adj, simile-meaning.adj),
% and the first to extend a table from single-valued to many-valued per key
% rather than adding new keys — the same reverse-lookup shape `flame_color.adj`
% already established, applied in the OTHER direction (many `example` values
% recalled for one bound `kingdom`).
%
% The envelope is now a FRAMING span — "The traditional five kingdoms are
% Animalia, Plantae, Fungi, Protista, and Monera." — what defends the table as
% a whole, plus a `cites` for the six-kingdom split, which is what makes
% `bacteria` a kingdom key at all. Until #14986 the envelope held the animalia
% "Examples:" line, so every answer in every kingdom quoted the same cell:
% ask for a fungus and the citation was a sentence about humans and sponges.
% (See ../README.md; ADJ-TABLES.md RS-5e; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table kingdom_example {
    columns kingdom, example

    row (animalia, humans) { source "Examples: Humans, birds, crustaceans, sponges" }
    row (animalia, birds) { source "Examples: Humans, birds, crustaceans, sponges" }
    row (animalia, crustaceans) { source "Examples: Humans, birds, crustaceans, sponges" }
    row (animalia, sponges) { source "Examples: Humans, birds, crustaceans, sponges" }
    row (plantae, flowers) { source "Examples: Flowers, grasses, conifers, ferns, mosses" }
    row (plantae, grasses) { source "Examples: Flowers, grasses, conifers, ferns, mosses" }
    row (plantae, conifers) { source "Examples: Flowers, grasses, conifers, ferns, mosses" }
    row (plantae, ferns) { source "Examples: Flowers, grasses, conifers, ferns, mosses" }
    row (plantae, mosses) { source "Examples: Flowers, grasses, conifers, ferns, mosses" }
    row (fungi, mushrooms) { source "Examples: Mushrooms, yeast, molds, rusts" }
    row (fungi, yeast) { source "Examples: Mushrooms, yeast, molds, rusts" }
    row (fungi, molds) { source "Examples: Mushrooms, yeast, molds, rusts" }
    row (protista, amoebas) { source "Examples: Amoebas, diatoms, dinoflagellates, ciliates, slime molds, single-celled algae" }
    row (protista, diatoms) { source "Examples: Amoebas, diatoms, dinoflagellates, ciliates, slime molds, single-celled algae" }
    row (protista, dinoflagellates) { source "Examples: Amoebas, diatoms, dinoflagellates, ciliates, slime molds, single-celled algae" }
    row (protista, ciliates) { source "Examples: Amoebas, diatoms, dinoflagellates, ciliates, slime molds, single-celled algae" }
    row (protista, slime_molds) { source "Examples: Amoebas, diatoms, dinoflagellates, ciliates, slime molds, single-celled algae" }
    row (protista, single_celled_algae) { source "Examples: Amoebas, diatoms, dinoflagellates, ciliates, slime molds, single-celled algae" }
    row (bacteria, cyanobacteria) { source "Examples: Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria, cyanobacteria, actinobacteria" }
    row (bacteria, gram_positive_bacteria) { source "Examples: Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria, cyanobacteria, actinobacteria" }
    row (bacteria, gram_negative_bacteria) { source "Examples: Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria, cyanobacteria, actinobacteria" }
    row (bacteria, actinobacteria) { source "Examples: Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria, cyanobacteria, actinobacteria" }

    source "The traditional five kingdoms are Animalia, Plantae, Fungi, Protista, and Monera."
    locator "https://sciencenotes.org/kingdoms-of-life-in-biology/"
    trust consensus

    % `bacteria` is a kingdom only in the SIX-kingdom system, which this
    % second span is what states. Without it the envelope would name five
    % kingdoms, one of which (Monera) is not a key in this table, while
    % the key that IS here (bacteria) went unnamed.
    cites "The six-kingdom system separates Monera into Bacteria and Archaea." locator "https://sciencenotes.org/kingdoms-of-life-in-biology/"
}
